   404	 Since each $G^*+i$ can be obtained from $G+i$ by (performing the same operation of) inserting $\infty_2$ along the edge $e_i\in E(G+i)$, it follows that
   405	 $\cG^*$ is a $2$-factorization of $K_{2a+2}\setminus I$ into copies of $G^*$.
   406	 Hence, $\cG^*$ is a $2$-pyramidal solution of $OP(2k+2, \;^2\ell_1,  \ldots,\;^2 \ell_u)$. 
   407	 Since the operations performed to obtain $\cG^*$ do not involve edges of $M$, 
   408	 then $\cG^*$ satisfies the matching property, with respect to $M$, the two distinct graphs $G^*$ and $(G')^*$, and the halving $H'$ of $(G')^*$, thus showing the assertion for $\epsilon=2$.
   409	\end{proof}
